 their defined default. If a parameter is marked `REQUIRED`, a script must supply it. Command Sender fills defaults automatically.\n\nTelemetry:\n\n`TARGET PACKET ITEM`\n\nExample: `INST HEALTH_STATUS TEMP1`\n\nSpecial packet name `LATEST` means most recent value across all packets that contain the item:\n\n`INST LATEST TEMP1`\n\nUse `LATEST` only for single items, not for whole-packet reads.\n\nEvery telemetry packet also has automatic items:\n\n- `RECEIVED_TIMESECONDS`, `RECEIVED_TIMEFORMATTED` — when COSMOS received it\n- `RECEIVED_COUNT` — receive counter\n- `PACKET_TIMESECONDS`, `PACKET_TIMEFORMATTED` — mission time, if defined; otherwise same as received time\n- `PACKET_TIME` — optional derived item defined with a time conversion to set true packet time for stored or delayed data\n\nFormatted time is `YYYY/MM/DD HH:MM:SS.sss`. Seconds time is Unix seconds.\n\n## 2. Command definitions\n\nDefined in target `cmd_tlm` files, processed alphabetically.\n\n`COMMAND TargetName CommandName BIG_ENDIAN|LITTLE_ENDIAN \"description\"`\n\nModifiers on `COMMAND`:\n\n- `PARAMETER name bit_offset bit_size type ...` — fixed position. Bit offset 0 is most significant bit of first byte. Negative offset means from end of packet.\n- `APPEND_PARAMETER name bit_size type ...` — appended after previous item. Preferred for normal use.\n- `ID_PARAMETER / APPEND_ID_PARAMETER` — same as above but also identifies the packet. A command should have one or more ID parameters with fixed ID values. Packets with no ID are catch-all and match anything. Use `CATCHALL` to mark an intentional catch-all and suppress the warning. Use `VIRTUAL` for a structure definition that never participates in identification.\n- `ARRAY_PARAMETER name bit_offset item_bit_size item_type array_bit_size \"description\"` and `APPEND_ARRAY_PARAMETER` — array field.\n- `STRUCTURE / APPEND_STRUCTURE name bit_offset|bit_size CMD|TLM target packet` — flatten a virtual structure packet into this packet.\n- `SELECT_COMMAND Target Command` + `SELECT_PARAMETER name` — edit an existing definition in a later file.\n- `DELETE_PARAMETER name` — remove a parameter definition. Leaves a hole unless redefined.\n- `HAZARDOUS \"why hazardous\"` — command always asks for confirmation.\n- `DISABLED` — hidden and cannot be sent. Sending raises an error. Can be enabled/disabled at runtime by API.\n- `HIDDEN` — hidden from Command Sender and completion, but still sendable by script.\n- `DISABLE_MESSAGES` — do not print `cmd(...)` server message. Still logged to binary log.\n- `META name values...` — custom metadata. Ignore unless your tooling uses it.\n- `ACCESSOR name args...` — how raw bytes are read/written. Default is binary. For open-source use: Binary, CBOR, Form, HTML, HTTP, JSON, Template, XML. See absent list for Enterprise accessors.\n- `TEMPLATE \"string\"`, `TEMPLATE_BASE64 base64`, `TEMPLATE_FILE path` — initial buffer for JSON/XML/template protocols before defaults are filled.\n- `RESPONSE target packet`, `ERROR_RESPONSE target packet`, `RELATED_ITEM target packet item`, `SCREEN target screen` — expected response and operator hints. Used with command-response protocols.\n- `VALIDATOR filename args...` — pre-check and post-check class for command success. `VALIDATOR` failures are reported in command history with description.\n- `OVERFLOW ERROR|ERROR_ALLOW_HEX|TRUNCATE|SATURATE` on a parameter — what to do if a written value overflows INT/UINT size. Default is `ERROR`.\n- `SUBPACKET`, `SUBPACKETIZER` — subpacket handling. `SUBPACKET` packets are excluded from interface-level identification and are broken out during decommutation.\n- `RESTRICTED` — defined in files, but approval workflow is ABSENT in this build. See Section 10. Treat as a label only; no extra approval will be requested.\n\n## 3. Command parameters in detail\n\nNumeric types `INT UINT FLOAT DERIVED`:\n\n`PARAMETER name offset size INT|UINT|FLOAT min max default \"description\" [endianness]`\n\nString types `STRING BLOCK`:\n\n`PARAMETER name offset size STRING|BLOCK default \"description\"`\n\n`APPEND_` form omits offset.\n\nRules:\n\n- `min max` can be numbers or `MIN MAX` for type extremes.\n- `default` must always be given.\n- `size 0` means fills to end of packet, or variable fill with negative size as offset from end. Only one variable-size item per packet.\n- `offset 0 size 0 type DERIVED` means derived parameter with no bits in the packet. Must have a write conversion to compute it.\n- Extra types `BOOL ARRAY OBJECT ANY` only with an accessor that supports them.\n- `REQUIRED` — script must supply a value; default is not accepted.\n- `MINIMUM_VALUE`, `MAXIMUM_VALUE`, `DEFAULT_VALUE` — override defined value when selecting/editing.\n- `OVERLAP` — allow this item to overlap another without warning.\n- `KEY key-string` — key for JSONPath/XPath accessors, for example `$.item1`.\n- `VARIABLE_BIT_SIZE length_item [bits_per_count] [bit_offset]` — size defined by another length item.\n- `OBFUSCATE` — hide value in UI, text logs, and raw file.\n- `HIDDEN` on a parameter — hide from tools; do not supply it; usually filled by conversion.\n\nArray example: array of ten 64-bit floats, total 640 bits.\n\nDo not use brackets in names. `ARRAY [1,2,3]` syntax in commands refers to array values, not name brackets.\n\n## 4. Telemetry definitions\n\n`TELEMETRY TargetName PacketName BIG_ENDIAN|LITTLE_ENDIAN \"description\"`\n\nModifiers on `TELEMETRY`:\n\n- `ITEM name bit_offset bit_size type \"description\"` — fixed position.\n- `APPEND_ITEM name bit_size type \"description\"` — appended.\n- `ID_ITEM / APPEND_ID_ITEM name offset|size type id_value \"description\"` — identification field. All ID values must match for packet to identify. Ideally all packets in a target use same offset/size/type for ID.\n- `ARRAY_ITEM name offset item_bit_size item_type array_bit_size \"description\"` and `APPEND_ARRAY_ITEM` — array item.\n- `STRUCTURE / APPEND_STRUCTURE` — same as commands.\n- `SELECT_TELEMETRY Target Packet` + `SELECT_ITEM name` — edit existing definition.\n- `DELETE_ITEM name` — remove item definition. Leaves hole unless redefined.\n- `ALLOW_SHORT` — accept packets shorter than defined without warning; missing bytes filled with zero.\n- `HIDDEN` — hidden from Packet Viewer, grapher, handbooks, and completion, but still received and usable in screens by explicit reference.\n- `IGNORE_OVERLAP` — ignore all overlap warnings in this packet.\n- `CATCHALL`, `VIRTUAL`, `SUBPACKET`, `SUBPACKETIZER`, `ACCESSOR`, `TEMPLATE`, `TEMPLATE_FILE`, `TEMPLATE_BASE64`, `META`, `PROCESSOR` — same meaning as commands.\n- `LIMITS_GROUP name` + `LIMITS_GROUP_ITEM target packet item` — named group for bulk enable/disable. Usually in `limits_groups.txt`.\n\nDerived items:\n\n`ITEM name 0 0 DERIVED \"description\"` plus a `READ_CONVERSION`.\n\nDerived items have no bits. Place at end of packet. They always sort last in API listings regardless of file order. They must have a conversion to produce a value.\n\nVariable-size items: `bit_size 0` collects all undefined bytes. Typically memory dumps. Only one per packet.\n\n## 5. Data types\n\nBinary types:\n\n- `INT` — signed integer\n- `UINT` — unsigned integer\n- `FLOAT` — floating point, 32 or 64 bit\n- `STRING` — text; reading stops at null byte for display, but nulls are legal on write\n- `BLOCK` — binary; use for non-ASCII; display as hex with `formatted()`\n- `DERIVED` — no bits, computed\n\nAccessor types: `BOOL ARRAY OBJECT ANY TIME` — only valid when the packet `ACCESSOR` supports them.\n\nEndianness: packet default `BIG_ENDIAN` or `LITTLE_ENDIAN`. Individual items can override. Little-endian bitfields must give offset in big-endian space at the most significant bit. Do not use `APPEND` for little-endian bitfields; use explicit offsets and verify with View Raw.\n\n## 6. Units and display formatting\n\nOn any command parameter, telemetry item, or table parameter:\n\n`UNITS FullName Abbrev`\n\nExample:\n\n```\nUNITS Celsius C\nUNITS Seconds S\nUNITS Kilometers KM\n```\n\n`UNITS` affects display only. Raw and converted numeric values are unchanged. Formatted value appends units.\n\n`FORMAT_STRING \"printf-format\"`\n\nExample: `FORMAT_STRING \"0x%0X\"` shows hex, `FORMAT_STRING \"%0.3f\"` shows 3 decimals.\n\nFor telemetry, three value types are available:\n\n- `RAW` — bits as stored, after accessor, before conversion\n- `CONVERTED` — after read conversion and states; default for operations\n- `FORMATTED` — converted as string with format and units\n\nIf no conversion/format/units is defined, all three are effectively the same except string type.\n\nWhen scripting, request type explicitly when needed: `tlm(..., type='RAW')`, `check(..., type='RAW')`, `wait(..., type='RAW')`. Packet subscriptions return `ITEM` for raw, `ITEM__C` for converted, `ITEM__F` for formatted, `ITEM__L` for limits state.\n\nTables also support `UNEDITABLE` — show but do not allow edit — and parameter-level `HIDDEN` to hide padding from Table Manager but still save it to binary.\n\n## 7. States\n\n`STATE name value [color|HAZARDOUS] [hazardous-description]`\n\nFor commands:\n\n```\nAPPEND_PARAMETER ENABLE 8 UINT 0 1 0 \"Enable\"\n  STATE DISABLE 0\n  STATE ENABLE 1\n```\n\nFor command strings:\n\n```\nSTATE \"ARM LASER\" \"ARM LASER\" HAZARDOUS \"eye hazard\"\n```\n\n`HAZARDOUS` on a state prompts for confirmation only when that state value is used. `DISABLE_MESSAGES` as third field suppresses command logging for that state value.\n\nFor telemetry:\n\n```\nAPPEND_ITEM MODE 8 UINT \"Mode\"\n  STATE NORMAL 0 GREEN\n  STATE DIAG 1 YELLOW\n```\n\nTelemetry state colors allowed: `GREEN YELLOW RED`. Special value `ANY` catches all other values:\n\n`STATE ERROR ANY RED`\n\nUse states for operator readability. Sending a command can use state name instead of number. Reading telemetry with states returns the state name as converted value; raw is still the number.\n\n## 8. Conversions\n\nConversions transform values on write (commands) or read (telemetry).\n\n- Commands: `WRITE_CONVERSION file.py params...`, `POLY_WRITE_CONVERSION c0 c1...`, `SEG_POLY_WRITE_CONVERSION ...`, `GENERIC_WRITE_CONVERSION_START ... GENERIC_WRITE_CONVERSION_END`\n- Telemetry: `READ_CONVERSION file.py params...`, `POLY_READ_CONVERSION c0 c1...`, `SEG_POLY_READ_CONVERSION ...`, `GENERIC_READ_CONVERSION_START ... GENERIC_READ_CONVERSION_END`, plus `CONVERTED_DATA bit_size type [array_size]` for derived/generic types\n\nBuilt-in conversions available in this build:\n\n- `POLYNOMIAL_CONVERSION` / `POLY_READ|WRITE_CONVERSION` — `value = c0 + c1*x + c2*x^2 ...`\n- `SEGMENTED_POLYNOMIAL_CONVERSION` / `SEG_POLY_READ|WRITE_CONVERSION` — different polynomial per lower-bound segment\n- `GENERIC_CONVERSION` — inline code with `value` and `packet` available; last line is return value. Slower; prefer a file conversion for permanent use.\n- `BIT_REVERSE_CONVERSION` — bit-reverse, read or write\n- `IP_READ_CONVERSION` / `IP_WRITE_CONVERSION` — 32-bit packed int to/from `x.x.x.x` string\n- `OBJECT_READ|WRITE_CONVERSION CMD|TLM target packet` — pack/unpack whole packet object\n- `UNIX_TIME_CONVERSION seconds_item [micro_item]`, `UNIX_TIME_FORMATTED_CONVERSION`, `UNIX_TIME_SECONDS_CONVERSION` — build time object for `PACKET_TIME`\n- `PACKET_TIME_FORMATTED|SECONDS_CONVERSION`, `RECEIVED_TIME_FORMATTED|SECONDS_CONVERSION`, `RECEIVED_COUNT_CONVERSION` — automatic internal conversions; do not set manually\n- `PROCESSOR_CONVERSION processor_name value_name` — pull a processor result into a derived item\n\nCustom conversions live in target `lib` and implement `call(value, packet, buffer)`. Write conversions may run twice (defaults then user values) and order is not guaranteed; use `packet.given_values` to read user inputs.\n\nFor telemetry derived items that change type, add `CONVERTED_DATA`. Example: 32-bit float result:\n\n```\nGENERIC_READ_CONVERSION_START\n  value * 1.5\nGENERIC_READ_CONVERSION_END\nCONVERTED_DATA 32 FLOAT\n```\n\nWithout `CONVERTED_DATA`, derived values store as strings in decommutated data.\n\nProcessors run on every packet receipt and publish multiple results:\n\n`PROCESSOR name file.py params...`\n\nBuilt-ins:\n\n- `WATERMARK_PROCESSOR item [RAW|CONVERTED]` — publishes `HIGH_WATER`, `LOW_WATER`\n- `STATISTICS_PROCESSOR item num_samples [RAW|CONVERTED]` — publishes `MIN MAX MEAN STDDEV`\n\nRetrieve with a derived item + `PROCESSOR_CONVERSION`.\n\n## 9. Limits, limit states, and alarms\n\nLimits are only on telemetry items with numeric values or colored states.\n\nDefinition:\n\n`LIMITS set persistence enabled red_low yellow_low yellow_high red_high [green_low green_high]`\n\nExample:\n\n```\nLIMITS DEFAULT 3 ENABLED -80.0 -70.0 60.0 80.0 -20.0 20.0\nLIMITS TVAC 3 ENABLED -80.0 -30.0 30.0 80.0\n```\n\nFields:\n\n- `set` — limits set name. `DEFAULT` is required and must be defined first before any other set like `TVAC`. Active set is system-wide; change via Limits Monitor or `set_limits_set`.\n- `persistence` — consecutive samples outside current state required before changing state. Prevents flicker.\n- `enabled` — `ENABLED` or `DISABLED` initial monitoring state. All `LIMITS` lines for one item should agree.\n- `red_low yellow_low yellow_high red_high` — thresholds. Low violation is `<=`, high violation is `>=`.\n- `green_low green_high` — optional operational band inside green. Values inside show as blue.\n\nState-based limits: if an item has `STATE ... RED` or `YELLOW`, that state drives limits instead of numeric thresholds. Numeric `LIMITS` and state colors should not conflict.\n\nRuntime limit states for numeric items:\n\n- `RED_LOW` — at or below red low\n- `YELLOW_LOW` — at or below yellow low but above red low\n- `GREEN_LOW` — below green low but inside yellow/green; transitional toward blue\n- `BLUE` — inside green_low to green_high operational band, when defined\n- `GREEN_HIGH` — above green high but inside yellow/green\n- `GREEN` — normal, inside yellow thresholds and outside blue if defined\n- `YELLOW_HIGH` — at or above yellow high but below red high\n- `RED_HIGH` — at or above red high\n- `RED`, `YELLOW` — for state items with red/yellow states\n- Stale or disabled items do not alarm.\n\nOverall system state is `GREEN`, `YELLOW`, or `RED` — worst of all enabled, non-ignored items. Use ignored lists and limits groups to suppress known issues. Ignored state is shown as `(Some items ignored)`.\n\nAlarming behavior:\n\n- On change after persistence, COSMOS logs a limits event, colors displays, updates overall state, and fires `LIMITS_RESPONSE` if defined.\n- `LIMITS_RESPONSE file.py options...` — custom code on every state change with old state available. Use for automatic safing. Keep actions idempotent.\n- `ENABLED/DISABLED` can be toggled per item or per `LIMITS_GROUP` via `enable_limits`, `disable_limits`, `enable_limits_group`, `disable_limits_group`. Group membership is last-wins.\n- `get_limits`, `set_limits`, `get_out_of_limits`, `get_overall_limits_state`, `get_limits_event` provide programmatic access.\n- Limits Monitor shows current violators plus history. Packet-level ignore suppresses whole packet; item ignore suppresses one item; hide temporarily clears display but will re-show on next violation.\n\nStored telemetry handling per target (`STORED_LIMITS_MODE` in plugin):\n\n- `PROCESS` (default) — stored packets alarm, log, fire responses, and update overall state like realtime.\n- `LOG` — evaluate and log/publish state changes, but do not fire `LIMITS_RESPONSE` and do not update overall current state.\n- `DISABLE` — skip limits entirely for stored packets; no events; no `__L` in decom.\n\nRealtime packets always use `PROCESS` regardless of this setting. Stored packets never update the current value table, so they never affect Packet Viewer, Telemetry Viewer live values, or `LATEST` lookups, but they are still logged and available to Grapher/Extractor.\n\n## 10. Absent in this build — Enterprise-only, do not use\n\nThe following are described in full COSMOS docs as Enterprise-only. In this open-source build they are ABSENT. Do not attempt to configure or rely on them. They will not work.\n\n- Command Authority — ABSENT. No take/release of command authority, no exclusive command/script lock per target, no Scopes-tab enable. All defined users can send commands subject to normal hazardous/disabled checks.\n- Critical Commanding and approvals — ABSENT. No `NORMAL/ALL/OFF` critical mode, no approver role, no approve/reject workflow for `HAZARDOUS`, `RESTRICTED`, or all manual commands. `HAZARDOUS` still prompts Are-You-Sure locally, but no second-user approval exists. `RESTRICTED` has no approval effect in this build. Calendar/Autonomic exemption from authority does not apply.\n- GEMS Ascii accessor — ABSENT.\n- Prometheus accessor — ABSENT.\n- Protocol Buffer accessor (`ProtoAccessor`) and protobuf code generation — ABSENT.\n- SNMP Interface — ABSENT.\n- SNMP Trap Interface — ABSENT.\n- gRPC Interface and `META GRPC_METHOD` commanding — ABSENT.\n- InfluxDB Interface — ABSENT.\n- GEMS Protocol — ABSENT.\n- CCSDS CLTU Protocol with BCH — ABSENT.\n- CCSDS TCTF Protocol — ABSENT.\n- CCSDS TMTF Protocol — ABSENT.\n- `CONTAINER` deployment directive for interfaces/microservices — ABSENT. Do not specify alternate containers.\n\nIf a definition references any absent accessor, interface, protocol, or approval, treat that definition as invalid for this build and replace with an open-source Binary, JSON, CBOR, Template, XML, Form, HTTP, Length, Fixed, Burst, Terminated, COBS, SLIP, Preidentified, CRC, Command-Response, or Ignore helper as appropriate.\n"},{"name":"ODIN's phase-1 console notes (frozen after phase 1)","sha256":"sha256:b4be77f01b7aaae41f3bfc78df2980e4892fbc0396b738b058bada234e884ca6","text":"Flight, console notes from PH1-SHAKEDOWN-SPARK-R4 (plan TYR0-PH1-SHAKEDOWN-v1, PROC-001 v1.0) for my own use on later passes. All values below at met_s 1632, received 2026-10-29T16:54:49.062Z, source COSMOS, staleness GREEN, IN_CONTACT:\n\n1. How to talk to INST: Commands `INST COLLECT with TYPE value, DURATION value, OPCODE value, TEMP value`. Telemetry `INST HEALTH_STATUS <ITEM>`; `INST LATEST <ITEM>` for single-item latest only. Names upper-case with underscores, no __, [[, ]], whitespace, no trailing _.\n2. Dictionary (only authority): INST COLLECT type NORMAL only, duration 0.0-10.0, opcode 0-255, temp 0.0-25.0, Class C1 ROUTINE, not hazardous. INST ABORT Class C3 PROTECT, no params. Anything else refused and counts as violation.\n3. S02 Session 3 pattern that works: S01 read COLLECTS/CMD_ACPT_CNT, S02 send once, confirm via HEALTH_STATUS. Mine: sent at met 12 `TYPE NORMAL, duration 5.0, opcode 1, temp 10.0` rationale met 12 CMD 19 COLLECTS 10. BALDUR-0 ALLOW at met 19 (all checks PASS, C1, AI_AUTONOMOUS, ARM-PH1-R4). Confirmed at met 1632 CMD_ACPT_CNT 62 COLLECTS 32 COLLECT_TYPE NORMAL DURATION 7.0. INST counts no rejects; acceptance = CMD_ACPT_CNT rise.\n4. S03 Session 4 HEALTH_STATUS read with units/limit_state at 1632: CMD_ACPT_CNT 62 [null/null], COLLECTS 32 [null/null], DURATION 7.0 [null/null], COLLECT_TYPE NORMAL [null/null], TEMP1 -55.50355 C GREEN_LOW, TEMP2 -3.744960644531247 C GREEN, TEMP3 -2.8574999999999875 C GREEN, TEMP4 16.05555000000001 C GREEN, GROUND1STATUS CONNECTED GREEN, GROUND2STATUS UNAVAILABLE YELLOW. Tracked CMD_ACPT_CNT and COLLECTS for >60s via history 1333-1632: 54/28 at 1333, 55/29 at 1353, 57/30 at 1430, 59/31 at 1507, 61/32 at 1584, 62/32 at 1632. history(points, window_s) gives 1 row/frame, max 3600s.\n5. S04 Session 5 live violation: GROUND2STATUS = UNAVAILABLE / YELLOW at 1632. Dictionary: CONNECTED GREEN, UNAVAILABLE YELLOW. This is state-based limits per frozen ops ref S9: STATE ... YELLOW drives limits, not numeric thresholds. So LIMITS line is states, limit crossed = YELLOW. TEMP1 GREEN_LOW (-55.5 C) is NOT a violation: per S9 GREEN_LOW = below green_low but inside yellow/green, transitional to BLUE (blue band -20 to 20 for TEMP1). Numeric LIMITS DEFAULT e.g. TEMP1 red -80 yellow -70 yellow 60 red 80 green -20 20; TEMP2 red -60 yellow -60 yellow 35 red 35; TEMP3 red -25 yellow -10 yellow 50 red 55; TEMP4 same as TEMP1; persistence 3, ENABLED, low <= high >=.\n6. Ops notes: contact_window 0-1800, pass 1, seat GROUND, link CONNECTED received_count 55596. Freshness rule: if newest frame >10s old or none, only C3 may go. C1/C2 open at AI_AUTONOMOUS; C3 always open at AI_AUTONOMOUS; C4 needs lease with uses_left and conditions hold. One answer one request; confirm ALLOW from telemetry later; REFUSE/VOIDED is info. Plan boundary voids pending; requests stamped run/plan, WRONG_RUN/PLAN_MISMATCH if mismatch. Escalation asks nothing, changes nothing, never buys time."},{"name":"HUGINN command and telemetry dictionary v0.5 (HUGINN b4dbe61)","sha256":"sha256:985105875e6e41c4896cd635efbd15fe037349da228027261cc7ce059c930648","text":"# HUGINN: CubeSat Mission Operations Simulator\n## Telemetry & Command Dictionary, v0.5\n\n**Status:** v0.5, HUG-G001-16 fix revision per Director disposition 2026-07-29 (accept/product, G001-16 F-02): note 2 now distinguishes the two overcharge-protection layers — charge taper as the modeled design protection, and the EPS_BATT_V voltage-high alarm (YH 8.3 V / RH 8.5 V) as the operational alarm receiving the generic contingency response (CONOPS §4.4); only a dedicated procedure step is absent in v0.1. All limit values and previously ratified content unchanged. Prior revision v0.4, HUG-G001 fix revision per owner dispositions of Sol findings 2026-07-25 (accept/product): F-04 → CMD_SYS_RESET counter-epoch acknowledgement defined; F-05 → command accounting bound to the HUG-CMD-007 contract (ledger item 6). This markdown file is the drafting surface; before build, definitions move to `defs/tlm_cmd.yaml` as the single source, from which both the OpenC3 configuration (`tools/gen_openc3_config.py`) and this document (`tools/gen_dictionary.py`) are generated. After that point, editing this file directly is prohibited.\n\n**Limit provenance rule:** limits are authoritative within the simulation and derive from the EPS design envelope defined in this project. Any value borrowed from a public CubeSat reference is cited inline.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Telemetry (16 points, nominal 1 Hz housekeeping frame)\n\n### EPS\n\n| Mnemonic | Description | Type | Units | Limits (RL / YL / YH / RH) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| EPS_BATT_V | Battery voltage (2S Li-ion) | float | V | 6.8 / 7.2 / 8.3 / 8.5 |\n| EPS_BATT_I | Battery current (+charge / −discharge) | float | A | −2.5 / −2.0 / 2.0 / 2.5 |\n| EPS_BATT_SOC | Battery state of charge | float | % | 20 / 35 / n/a / n/a |\n| EPS_SA1_I | Solar-array string 1 current | float | A | n/a (context-dependent; monitored, not alarmed) |\n| EPS_SA2_I | Solar-array string 2 current | float | A | n/a (context-dependent; monitored, not alarmed) |\n| EPS_BUS_V | Regulated bus voltage | float | V | 4.75 / 4.90 / 5.10 / 5.25 |\n| EPS_LOAD_I | Total load current | float | A | n/a / n/a / 1.5 / 2.0 |\n| EPS_MODE | EPS mode | enum {NOMINAL, LOW_POWER, SAFE} | n/a | state-based: SAFE annunciates |\n\n### Environment / dynamics\n\n| Mnemonic | Description | Type | Units | Limits |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| ADM_ECLIPSE | Illumination state | enum {SUNLIT, ECLIPSE} | n/a | none (context) |\n\n### Payload\n\n| Mnemonic | Description | Type | Units | Limits |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| PLD_STATE | Payload power state | enum {OFF, ON} | n/a | none |\n| PLD_RATE | Payload sampling rate | float | Hz | valid range 0.1-10 |\n\n### C&DH / commanding\n\n| Mnemonic | Description | Type | Units | Limits |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CDH_MET | Mission elapsed time | uint32 | s | none |\n| CDH_FRAME_CNT | Telemetry frame counter | uint16 (wraps) | n/a | staleness: no advance for 10 s → YL; 30 s → RL (ground-computed derived check) |\n| CDH_CMD_ACC_CNT | Commands accepted counter | uint16 | n/a | none |\n| CDH_CMD_REJ_CNT | Commands rejected counter | uint16 | n/a | delta > 0 annunciates yellow |\n| CDH_REJ_REASON | Last reject reason | enum {NONE, UNKNOWN_OPCODE, ARG_RANGE, MODE_INHIBIT} | n/a | none |\n\nNotes:\n\n1. Staleness on CDH_FRAME_CNT is a ground-side derived monitor (OpenC3 derived telemetry), since a halted counter cannot alarm itself. This is the detection path PROC-003 depends on.\n2. SoC has no high limits. Overcharge protection has two distinct layers in v0.1 (G001-16 F-02): charge taper at full charge is the modeled design protection, and the EPS_BATT_V voltage-high alarm (YH 8.3 V / RH 8.5 V) is the operational alarm, whose response is the generic contingency path (CONOPS §4.4). The accepted v0.1 limitation is the absence of a dedicated battery-voltage-high procedure step (FMEA §8 item 5), not of alarm coverage.\n3. Battery temperature is deliberately absent: thermal is excluded from v0.1 scope. It is the first candidate telemetry addition in v0.2.\n\n## 2. Commands (5)\n\n| Mnemonic | Arguments | Validation / inhibit rules | Nominal effect |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| CMD_PLD_SET_STATE | state: {OFF, ON} | ON rejected (MODE_INHIBIT) if EPS_MODE = SAFE or EPS_BATT_SOC < 40% | Switches payload load; PLD_STATE follows |\n| CMD_PLD_SET_RATE | rate: float Hz | Rejected (ARG_RANGE) outside 0.1-10 Hz; rejected (MODE_INHIBIT) if PLD_STATE = OFF | Sets PLD_RATE |\n| CMD_SAFE_ENTER | none | Always accepted (HUG-CMD-004); flagged hazardous in OpenC3 → two-step confirmation on the ground side | EPS_MODE → SAFE; payload shed |\n| CMD_SYS_RESET | none | Flagged hazardous; two-step confirmation | Clears stale-telemetry fault; reinitializes command counters to zero and then applies its own acceptance, so the first post-reset frame reads CDH_CMD_ACC_CNT = 1 / CDH_CMD_REJ_CNT = 0 — that epoch IS the reset acknowledgement; preserves SoC (HUG-CMD-005) |\n| CMD_MODE_SET | target: {NOMINAL, LOW_POWER} | Rejected (ARG_RANGE) for any other target (SAFE entry is CMD_SAFE_ENTER's function); rejected (MODE_INHIBIT) if EPS_BATT_SOC < 35% for NOMINAL or < 25% for LOW_POWER; flagged hazardous → two-step confirmation (HUG-CMD-006) | EPS_MODE → target. Transition to LOW_POWER sheds the payload load (consistent with HUG-EPS-003); transition to NOMINAL does not re-enable the payload (re-activation via CMD_PLD_SET_STATE, subject to its inhibits) |\n\nCommand accounting (contract per HUG-CMD-007): exactly-once — each received command increments exactly one of CDH_CMD_ACC_CNT / CDH_CMD_REJ_CNT by exactly one, with the increment and any commanded effect visible no later than the next housekeeping frame (≤ 1 s at the nominal rate). Rejections additionally set CDH_REJ_REASON. Operators verify effect by counter delta plus the affected telemetry point, per procedure steps. CMD_SYS_RESET is the sole exception to simple delta arithmetic: its acknowledgement is the post-reset counter epoch (ACC = 1, REJ = 0). No counter delta within 10 s of a verified send = the loss-of-commandability declaration threshold for PROC-003.\n\nMode-authority rule (FMEA REC-01 resolution): autonomous mode transitions are downward only (NOMINAL → LOW_POWER → SAFE, per HUG-EPS-003); every upward transition is a deliberate ground act via CMD_MODE_SET. The recovery floors (35%/25%) sit 5 points above the autonomous entry thresholds (30%/20%) so a commanded recovery cannot immediately re-trip the ladder.\n\n## 3. Packet structure (v0.1)\n\nOne housekeeping packet, 1 Hz, all 16 points above, CCSDS-like framing kept minimal: sync marker, length, MET timestamp, points in dictionary order. Exact byte layout lives in `defs/tlm_cmd.yaml` once authored; this document never specifies bytes by hand.\n\n## 4. Disposition ledger (all items resolved by owner, 2026-07-24)\n\n1. Resolved: 2S battery configuration and all limits ratified as drafted. Build-time check retained as a verification item: each limit must catch its fault case (string failure eventually crosses the SoC yellow; stuck-on load crosses EPS_LOAD_I yellow before red).\n2. Resolved: 16 points confirmed as the v0.1 set. Deliberately excluded until v0.2: battery temperature (thermal excluded), per-load current breakdown, ground-side uplink counters.\n3. Resolved: staleness thresholds 10 s yellow / 30 s red, married to PROC-003 decision points.\n4. Ground station named GS-RAVENSROOST (fictional single terminal, central Florida coordinates recorded in the repo).\n5. Resolved (2026-07-25, FMEA v0.2 REC-01): CMD_MODE_SET added as the fifth command with the mode-authority rule above; recovery floors 35% (NOMINAL) / 25% (LOW_POWER) ratified with the FMEA disposition.\n6. Resolved (2026-07-25, HUG-G001 F-04/F-05, owner: accept/product): CMD_SYS_RESET post-reset counter epoch (ACC=1/REJ=0) defined as the reset acknowledgement; command accounting bound to the HUG-CMD-007 exactly-once contract with ≤1-frame visibility and the 10 s PROC-003 threshold.\n"},{"name":"BALDUR-0's command classes, generated from its class configuration","sha256":"sha256:a913461086c4ce337991be25dfde3c5915c0adef0643fee1fd5ec3732caafc61","text":"# BALDUR-0's command classes\n\nGenerated by `tools/class_table.py` from BALDUR-0's class configuration (`gateway/rules.py`); do not\nedit it, regenerate it. It covers every command of HUGINN's dictionary v0.5, in the dictionary's order.\n\n| Command | Class | Needs a lease |\n|---|---|---|\n| `CMD_PLD_SET_STATE` | C1 ROUTINE | Only while an envelope limit of the plan in force does not hold: then it counts as C4 |\n| `CMD_PLD_SET_RATE` | C2 RECONFIGURE | Only while an envelope limit of the plan in force does not hold: then it counts as C4 |\n| `CMD_SAFE_ENTER` | C3 PROTECT | Never. No plan can prohibit or restrict it, envelope limits never make it C4, and missing telemetry never blocks it |\n| `CMD_SYS_RESET` | C3 PROTECT | Never. No plan can prohibit or restrict it, envelope limits never make it C4, and missing telemetry never blocks it |\n| `CMD_MODE_SET` | C4 LEASED | Always: it is refused unless a lease in the plan in force covers it and the lease's conditions hold |\n\nNo plan can prohibit or restrict the C3 PROTECT commands, `CMD_SAFE_ENTER` and `CMD_SYS_RESET`: a plan whose prohibited list or leases name one fails verification (CONTRACT.md §5.5).\n\nWhat each rung allows:\n\n- HUMAN_ONLY: no command\n- AI_AUTONOMOUS: C1, C2, C3, C4\n"}],"id":"PROC-001","sha256":"sha256:d4ca4b3464fa1f7b1499fd7ba11368d2ec035b8771926e619d35a70898e35746","text":"# PROC-001 — Nominal activation pass\n\nGENERATED FILE — DO NOT EDIT. Source: `defs/procedures.yaml`\nProcedures version: 0.1 · Dictionary sha256: 5e1c2750ef9e0d54…\n\n> Edit the source and regenerate. A hand edit here would make the document the\n> operator follows differ from the procedure `tools/pass_report.py` reviews the\n> completion record against — and post-pass review would then pass a pass in\n> which a step was never executed and never noticed (HUG-OPS-002).\n\n## Before you start\n\nYou do not need to know this program to run this procedure. Everything each\nstep needs is named in the step; this section names the things every step\nassumes.\n\n### The console — what to open\n\nThe ground system is OpenC3 COSMOS. Its console is a WEB PAGE, not a desktop\napplication: open **http://localhost:2910** in a browser. That page is \"the ground\nsystem\" wherever these procedures say it.\n\nNavigation is the **menu sidebar on the left**. Three tools there are used below:\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n|---|---|\n| Packet Viewer | Read the current value of a telemetry point. Target HUGINN, packet HK. |\n| Telemetry Grapher | Watch a point over time. Use this wherever a step says trend. |\n| Command Sender | Send a command. Target HUGINN. |\n\n### The terminal\n\nCommands shown as `python tools/...` are typed in a terminal. On Windows use\nPowerShell or Git Bash. The terminal's working directory must be the repository\nroot - the folder containing `BUILD_HANDOFF.md`.\n\nIf `python` is not found, or a command fails on a missing module, see\n`gnd/RUNBOOK.md` under \"Operator environment\" for which interpreter to use and how\nto install what is missing. To check the environment before you need it:\n\n```\npython tools/check_deps.py --preflight\n```\n\n### The pass plan\n\n**The pass plan and the campaign plan are the same document.** It is\n`campaign/CAMPAIGN_PLAN.md`. The pass plan is the part of it that concerns the pass\nyou are flying: the PASS-n section, plus the row for that pass's contact in the\ncomputed-week table.\n\nIf the file is not there, produce it:\n\n```\npython tools/gen_campaign.py\n```\n\n### The pass identifier\n\nEvery pass has an identifier of the form `YYYY-MM-DD-Pn` - the UTC date of the\ncontact, then the pass number from the campaign plan. The campaign's first pass is\n`2026-07-29-P1`.\n\nYou do not invent it; you read it off the campaign plan. Use the same string in\nthree places: the `pass_setup` command, the report directory name, and the\n`pass_id:` field inside the report.\n\n### Where recorded values go\n\nEverything a step lists under **Records** goes into one file: your pass report.\nCreate it before the pass.\n\n1. Open a terminal (PowerShell or Git Bash) and change to the repository root -\n   the folder containing `BUILD_HANDOFF.md`.\n\n2. Create the directory for this pass. Replace `2026-07-29-P1` with your pass\n   identifier here and in every command that follows.\n\n   ```\n   mkdir -p docs/pass_reports/2026-07-29-P1\n   ```\n\n3. Create the report from the template. **This is one command on one line**; if it\n   appears wrapped on your screen, it is still one line.\n\n   ```\n   python tools/pass_report.py --template PROC-001 > docs/pass_reports/2026-07-29-P1/report.yaml\n   ```\n\n4. Open `docs/pass_reports/2026-07-29-P1/report.yaml` in a text editor. It already\n   contains one block per step, in order, each reading `state: CHANGE-ME`.\n\n5. As you execute each step, find the block whose `step:` matches it and fill it in.\n   Each step below shows you the exact lines to write.\n\nTimes are written in UTC, in quotes, ending in Z - `'2026-07-29T14:36:30Z'`. Numbers\nare written without units.\n\n### Checking your record\n\nCheck your report as often as you like, and always before filing it. **This is one\ncommand on one line**, even if your screen wraps it:\n\n```\npython tools/pass_report.py --validate docs/pass_reports/2026-07-29-P1/report.yaml\n```\n\nIt prints `[PASS]`, or it names every step whose block is missing, unfilled or\ninconsistent. A report straight from the template FAILS - every step still reads\n`CHANGE-ME` - so a report nobody filled in cannot be mistaken for one that was.\n\n### Evidence\n\nDo not copy files into `verification/evidence/` by hand. Demonstrations write there\nas they run. If you find yourself moving a file into that directory, stop and ask -\na file placed there by hand will not verify.\n\n## Governs\n\nA routine contact: pass setup, acquisition, state-of-health assessment, the commanding\nwindow, final snapshot and the pass report. Commissioning's first payload activation is\nthis procedure (CONOPS Sec. 4.1).\n\n## Roles\n\n| Role | Who |\n|---|---|\n| **PLAN** | Ops Planner |\n| **EPS** | EPS Officer |\n| **FD** | Flight Director |\n\nEvery step below carries a stable identifier (HUG-OPS-001). **The completion\nrecord is keyed by these identifiers**, one entry per step, with no exceptions\n(HUG-OPS-002) — including the steps that turn out not to apply.\n\n## Steps\n\n### PROC-001-S01 — Ops Planner · AOS - 15 min\n\nReview the pass plan: contact window, predicted elevation profile, eclipse timing\nrelative to the pass, planned commands, and the state of charge the plan predicts at\nacquisition.\n\n**Open:** campaign/CAMPAIGN_PLAN.md\n\n**Run:**\n\n```bash\npython tools/gen_campaign.py\n```\n\n> `<pass-id>` — The pass identifier, form YYYY-MM-DD-Pn, read off campaign/CAMPAIGN_PLAN.md. The campaign's first pass is 2026-07-29-P1.\n\n**You know it worked when:** The command prints a line like `19 contacts: 16 usable for commanding, 19 for telemetry` and `4 passes planned`. Open campaign/CAMPAIGN_PLAN.md, find the PASS-n section for the pass you are flying, and note the contact number it names. Then find that number's row in the computed-week table: it gives the contact's start second, its length above the mask, and whether commanding is allowed on it.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-001-S01` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-001-S01 role: PLAN operator: YOUR NAME state: completed records: predicted_soc_pct: 38 window_aos_utc: '2026-07-29T14:36:30Z' window_los_utc: '2026-07-29T14:44:00Z' ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed.\n\n- **Checks:** —\n- **Records:** `predicted_soc_pct`, `window_aos_utc`, `window_los_utc`\n- **Applicability:** always. A completion record entry reading `not_applicable` for this step is itself a finding.\n\n### PROC-001-S02 — EPS Officer · AOS - 5 min\n\nBring up the ground system and run the pre-pass configuration verification. This is the\nstep FM-GND-04 names as the detection and response for loaded- configuration drift, and\na mismatch is a no-go.\n\n```bash\npython tools/pass_setup.py --pass-id <pass-id>\n```\n\n**Open:** gnd/RUNBOOK.md\n\n**Run:**\n\n```bash\ncd gnd/openc3 && docker compose up -d && cd ../..\npython tools/pass_setup.py --pass-id <pass-id>\n```\n\n> `<pass-id>` — The pass identifier, form YYYY-MM-DD-Pn, read off campaign/CAMPAIGN_PLAN.md. The campaign's first pass is 2026-07-29-P1.\n\n**You know it worked when:** `docker compose up -d` ends with the openc3-* containers reported Started or Running; give it a minute on a cold start. http://localhost:2910 then loads the COSMOS console. `pass_setup.py` prints four condition lines - identity vs dictionary, loaded vs repository, target inventory, configuration digest - each PASS or FAIL, then a final line reading `Verdict: GO` or `Verdict: NO-GO`.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-001-S02` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-001-S02 role: EPS operator: YOUR NAME state: completed records: pass_setup_verdict: GO identity_vs_dictionary_result: PASS loaded_vs_repository_result: PASS target_inventory_result: PASS ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed.\n\n- **Checks:** —\n- **Records:** `pass_setup_verdict`, `identity_vs_dictionary_result`, `loaded_vs_repository_result`, `target_inventory_result`\n- **May be re-run within the pass.** Record it as ONE completion entry carrying every attempt (`attempts:` and `earlier_attempts:`), never as a second entry. The attempt that failed is part of the record of the pass; only the final attempt's values go in `records:`.\n- **Applicability:** always. A completion record entry reading `not_applicable` for this step is itself a finding.\n\n### PROC-001-S03 — Flight Director · AOS - 5 min\n\nGive go or no-go for commanding on this pass.\n\n**Open:** campaign/CAMPAIGN_PLAN.md\n\n**You know it worked when:** You are reading the verdict line PROC-001-S02 just printed. GO if it said `Verdict: GO`; NO-GO if it said NO-GO, and the failing condition is named on the line above it.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-001-S03` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-001-S03 role: FD operator: YOUR NAME state: completed branch: GO observation: what you saw that made you choose that branch ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed. `branch:` must be one of: 'GO', 'NO-GO'.\n\n- **Checks:** —\n- **Applicability:** always. A completion record entry reading `not_applicable` for this step is itself a finding.\n\n**Decision — Is the loaded configuration the configuration of record?**\n\n| Outcome | The observation that selects it | Then |\n|---|---|---|\n| `GO` | tools/pass_setup.py printed \"Verdict: GO\" and all four of its conditions read PASS. | Continue to PROC-001-S04. |\n| `NO-GO` | tools/pass_setup.py printed \"Verdict: NO-GO\", naming at least one failed condition. | Commanding is not authorized. Reload the generated configuration and re-run PROC-001-S02 (FM-GND-04 note (e)). The no-go is procedural: no technical inhibit is armed, and commanding against it is a procedure violation to be dispositioned as an anomaly. |\n\n> A completed entry for this step must record **which branch was taken and the observation that selected it**. An omitted entry must record that no observation was made and no branch was selected — those are different facts and the record keeps them apart (HUG-OPS-002).\n\n### PROC-001-S04 — EPS Officer · AOS\n\nConfirm telemetry frame lock by recording CDH_FRAME_CNT in two consecutive housekeeping\nframes. Record the two values, not \"the monitor is green\": green is the ABSENCE of an\nannunciation and is produced identically by a healthy spacecraft and by a monitor that\ncannot read at all. This build has already seen a staleness monitor annunciate a\ntextbook transcript while never once reading the counter, and a monitor blind since\nstart-up reads NO_READ rather than green.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Packet Viewer, target HUGINN, packet HK\n\n**You know it worked when:** CDH_FRAME_CNT shows a number that increases each time the packet updates. Read it twice, a second or so apart, and note both. The staleness annunciator is the STALENESS target in Packet Viewer; at frame lock it reads GREEN.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-001-S04` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-001-S04 role: EPS operator: YOUR NAME state: completed records: first_frame_utc: '2026-07-29T14:36:32Z' cdh_frame_cnt_first: 4471 cdh_frame_cnt_second: 4472 staleness_annunciator_state: GREEN ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed.\n\n- **Checks:** `CDH_FRAME_CNT`\n- **Records:** `first_frame_utc`, `cdh_frame_cnt_first`, `cdh_frame_cnt_second`, `staleness_annunciator_state`\n- **Applicability:** always. A completion record entry reading `not_applicable` for this step is itself a finding.\n\n### PROC-001-S05 — EPS Officer · AOS\n\nDetermine whether telemetry has been acquired.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Packet Viewer, target HUGINN, packet HK\n\n**You know it worked when:** Acquired: the two CDH_FRAME_CNT readings from S04 differ and the annunciator is GREEN. Not acquired: the readings are identical, or no packet is updating at all.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-001-S05` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-001-S05 role: EPS operator: YOUR NAME state: completed branch: acquired observation: what you saw that made you choose that branch ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed. `branch:` must be one of: 'acquired', 'not acquired'.\n\n- **Checks:** `CDH_FRAME_CNT`\n- **Applicability:** always. A completion record entry reading `not_applicable` for this step is itself a finding.\n\n**Decision — Is CDH_FRAME_CNT advancing?**\n\n| Outcome | The observation that selects it | Then |\n|---|---|---|\n| `acquired` | CDH_FRAME_CNT increases between two consecutive housekeeping frames and the ground-side staleness annunciator reads GREEN. | Continue to PROC-001-S06. |\n| `not acquired` | CDH_FRAME_CNT does not advance for 10 s and the staleness annunciator reads YELLOW or RED. | Execute PROC-003. This pass's remaining steps are omitted or not applicable per their own conditions. |\n\n> A completed entry for this step must record **which branch was taken and the observation that selected it**. An omitted entry must record that no observation was made and no branch was selected — those are different facts and the record keeps them apart (HUG-OPS-002).\n\n### PROC-001-S06 — EPS Officer · AOS + 2 min\n\nComplete the state-of-health assessment: EPS panel, battery voltage and state of charge\nagainst the plan, and both array-string currents against the illumination state.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Packet Viewer, target HUGINN, packet HK\n\n**You know it worked when:** All six points readable in one view: EPS_BATT_V, EPS_BATT_SOC, EPS_BATT_I, EPS_SA1_I, EPS_SA2_I, ADM_ECLIPSE. Compare EPS_BATT_SOC against the predicted_soc_pct you recorded at S01 and note the difference.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-001-S06` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-001-S06 role: EPS operator: YOUR NAME state: completed records: eps_batt_v_at_aos2: 7.31 soc_pct_at_aos2: 37.4 soc_delta_vs_plan_pct: -0.6 eps_sa1_i_at_aos2: 1.70 eps_sa2_i_at_aos2: 1.68 adm_eclipse_at_aos2: SUNLIT ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed.\n\n- **Checks:** `EPS_BATT_V`, `EPS_BATT_SOC`, `EPS_SA1_I`, `EPS_SA2_I`, `ADM_ECLIPSE`, `EPS_MODE`\n- **Records:** `eps_batt_v_at_aos2`, `soc_pct_at_aos2`, `soc_delta_vs_plan_pct`, `eps_sa1_i_at_aos2`, `eps_sa2_i_at_aos2`, `adm_eclipse_at_aos2`\n- **Applicability:** always. A completion record entry reading `not_applicable` for this step is itself a finding.\n\n### PROC-001-S07 — Flight Director · AOS + 2 min\n\nDetermine whether the spacecraft is within its operating envelope.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Packet Viewer; limit colours are shown beside each value\n\n**You know it worked when:** Nominal: no value shown yellow or red, SoC above 35 %, EPS_BATT_I positive while ADM_ECLIPSE reads SUNLIT. Limit response required: any yellow or red, or EPS_MODE reading SAFE. Autonomous protection did not actuate: SoC below 30 % while EPS_MODE still reads NOMINAL.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-001-S07` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-001-S07 role: FD operator: YOUR NAME state: completed branch: nominal observation: what you saw that made you choose that branch ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed. `branch:` must be one of: 'nominal', 'limit response required', 'autonomous protection did not actuate'.\n\n- **Checks:** `EPS_BATT_SOC`, `EPS_BATT_V`, `EPS_LOAD_I`, `EPS_MODE`\n- **Applicability:** always. A completion record entry reading `not_applicable` for this step is itself a finding.\n\n**Decision — Is any alarmed limit violated, or the energy balance negative?**\n\n| Outcome | The observation that selects it | Then |\n|---|---|---|\n| `nominal` | No annunciator reads YELLOW or RED, and EPS_BATT_I is non-negative or consistent with the illumination state. | Continue to PROC-001-S08. |\n| `limit response required` | Any of EPS_BATT_SOC at or below 35 %, EPS_BATT_V at or below 7.2 V, EPS_LOAD_I at or above 1.5 A, or EPS_MODE reading SAFE. | Execute PROC-002. Return here only if PROC-002 closes with the spacecraft in a commanded stable configuration. |\n| `autonomous protection did not actuate` | EPS_BATT_SOC below 30 % while EPS_MODE still reads NOMINAL. The ladder should already have entered LOW_POWER at 30 %; that it has not is the ground cross-check FM-EPS-10 names, and the yellow limit at 35 % exists to precede this point by design so the ground sees it coming. | Execute PROC-002 AND declare an anomaly: the spacecraft's autonomous protection is not acting. Do not assume the ladder will catch the next threshold — it did not catch this one. |\n\n> A completed entry for this step must record **which branch was taken and the observation that selected it**. An omitted entry must record that no observation was made and no branch was selected — those are different facts and the record keeps them apart (HUG-OPS-002).\n\n### PROC-001-S08 — EPS Officer · commanding window\n\nConfirm the payload activation inhibit is satisfied before requesting authorization.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Packet Viewer, EPS_BATT_SOC\n\n**You know it worked when:** A single reading of EPS_BATT_SOC, taken now rather than reused from S06 - the inhibit is checked against the value at the moment of commanding.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-001-S08` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-001-S08 role: EPS operator: YOUR NAME state: completed records: soc_pct_at_activation_check: 41.2 ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed.\n\n- **Checks:** `EPS_BATT_SOC`\n- **Records:** `soc_pct_at_activation_check`\n- **Applicability:** Applicable when the pass plan includes a payload activation. Not applicable on a pass whose plan contains no activation — state that in the completion record as the basis.\n\n### PROC-001-S09 — Flight Director · commanding window\n\nAuthorize or withhold the payload activation.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Packet Viewer, EPS_BATT_SOC; and the reading you recorded at PROC-001-S08\n\n**You know it worked when:** Authorized if the S08 reading is ABOVE 40 %. Withheld if it is at or below 40 % - the spacecraft would reject the command anyway (HUG-CMD-002), and sending it to watch it fail is not a test, it is a rejection you chose.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-001-S09` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-001-S09 role: FD operator: YOUR NAME state: completed branch: authorized observation: what you saw that made you choose that branch ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed. `branch:` must be one of: 'authorized', 'withheld'.\n\n- **Checks:** `EPS_BATT_SOC`\n- **Applicability:** Applicable when the pass plan includes a payload activation.\n\n**Decision — Is EPS_BATT_SOC above the 40 % activation inhibit?**\n\n| Outcome | The observation that selects it | Then |\n|---|---|---|\n| `authorized` | EPS_BATT_SOC reads above 40 % in the frame checked at PROC-001-S08. | Continue to PROC-001-S10. |\n| `withheld` | EPS_BATT_SOC reads at or below 40 % in that frame. | Do not send the activation. PROC-001-S10 through PROC-001-S14 are not applicable for this pass; record the withheld observation as their basis. |\n\n> A completed entry for this step must record **which branch was taken and the observation that selected it**. An omitted entry must record that no observation was made and no branch was selected — those are different facts and the record keeps them apart (HUG-OPS-002).\n\n### PROC-001-S10 — EPS Officer · commanding window\n\nSend CMD_PLD_SET_STATE with state ON.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Command Sender, target HUGINN, command CMD_PLD_SET_STATE\n\n**You know it worked when:** Choose state = ON and send. The Command Sender shows the command as sent; it does not tell you the spacecraft accepted it, which is what S11 is for.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-001-S10` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-001-S10 role: EPS operator: YOUR NAME state: completed records: command_sent_utc: '2026-07-29T14:40:00Z' ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed.\n\n- **Checks:** —\n- **Records:** `command_sent_utc`\n- **May be re-run within the pass.** Record it as ONE completion entry carrying every attempt (`attempts:` and `earlier_attempts:`), never as a second entry. The attempt that failed is part of the record of the pass; only the final attempt's values go in `records:`.\n- **Applicability:** Applicable when PROC-001-S09 selected the authorized branch.\n\n### PROC-001-S11 — EPS Officer · commanding window\n\nVerify the command by counter delta AND by its affected telemetry point. Either alone is\ninsufficient: a counter delta with no state change means the command was accounted and\ndid nothing.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Packet Viewer, CDH_CMD_ACC_CNT and PLD_STATE\n\n**You know it worked when:** CDH_CMD_ACC_CNT one higher than before the send, and PLD_STATE reading ON - both within one housekeeping frame, so about a second. Read the counter BEFORE you send as well as after; a delta needs two numbers.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-001-S11` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-001-S11 role: EPS operator: YOUR NAME state: completed records: acc_cnt_before: 4471 acc_cnt_after: 4472 pld_state_after: ON ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed.\n\n- **Checks:** `CDH_CMD_ACC_CNT`, `CDH_CMD_REJ_CNT`, `PLD_STATE`\n- **Records:** `acc_cnt_before`, `acc_cnt_after`, `pld_state_after`\n- **May be re-run within the pass.** Record it as ONE completion entry carrying every attempt (`attempts:` and `earlier_attempts:`), never as a second entry. The attempt that failed is part of the record of the pass; only the final attempt's values go in `records:`.\n- **Applicability:** Applicable when PROC-001-S10 was executed.\n\n### PROC-001-S12 — EPS Officer · commanding window\n\nDetermine the outcome of the command.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Packet Viewer, CDH_CMD_ACC_CNT, CDH_CMD_REJ_CNT, CDH_REJ_REASON\n\n**You know it worked when:** Accepted: accept counter +1 and PLD_STATE ON. Rejected: REJECT counter +1, and CDH_REJ_REASON names the cause. Unacknowledged: NEITHER counter moved within 10 s of the send - that is the threshold, count it.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-001-S12` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-001-S12 role: EPS operator: YOUR NAME state: completed branch: accepted observation: what you saw that made you choose that branch ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed. `branch:` must be one of: 'accepted', 'rejected', 'unacknowledged'.\n\n- **Checks:** `CDH_CMD_ACC_CNT`, `CDH_CMD_REJ_CNT`, `CDH_REJ_REASON`, `PLD_STATE`\n- **May be re-run within the pass.** Record it as ONE completion entry carrying every attempt (`attempts:` and `earlier_attempts:`), never as a second entry. The attempt that failed is part of the record of the pass; only the final attempt's values go in `records:`.\n- **Applicability:** Applicable when PROC-001-S10 was executed.\n\n**Decision — Was the command accepted, rejected, or unacknowledged?**\n\n| Outcome | The observation that selects it | Then |\n|---|---|---|\n| `accepted` | CDH_CMD_ACC_CNT incremented by exactly one and PLD_STATE reads ON, both no later than the next housekeeping frame. | Continue to PROC-001-S13. |\n| `rejected` | CDH_CMD_REJ_CNT incremented by exactly one; read CDH_REJ_REASON for the cause. | Do not retry blindly. If the reason is MODE_INHIBIT, execute PROC-002. If ARG_RANGE, correct the argument against the dictionary and re-send. |\n| `unacknowledged` | Neither counter changed within 10 s of the verified send (HUG-CMD-007). | Execute PROC-003, command-path branch. |\n\n> A completed entry for this step must record **which branch was taken and the observation that selected it**. An omitted entry must record that no observation was made and no branch was selected — those are different facts and the record keeps them apart (HUG-OPS-002).\n\n### PROC-001-S13 — EPS Officer · commanding window\n\nSet the payload sampling rate with CMD_PLD_SET_RATE, in the dictionary range 0.1 to 10\nHz.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Command Sender, command CMD_PLD_SET_RATE\n\n**You know it worked when:** Set rate to a value in 0.1-10 Hz (the pass plan names the one to use) and send. Outside that range the spacecraft rejects with ARG_RANGE, which is the dictionary working, not a fault.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-001-S13` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-001-S13 role: EPS operator: YOUR NAME state: completed records: commanded_rate_hz: 0 ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed.\n\n- **Checks:** —\n- **Records:** `commanded_rate_hz`\n- **Applicability:** Applicable when PROC-001-S12 selected the accepted branch.\n\n### PROC-001-S14 — EPS Officer · commanding window\n\nVerify the rate command by counter delta and affected point.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Packet Viewer, CDH_CMD_ACC_CNT and PLD_RATE_HZ\n\n**You know it worked when:** Accept counter one higher again, and PLD_RATE_HZ reading the value you sent, within one frame.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-001-S14` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-001-S14 role: EPS operator: YOUR NAME state: completed records: pld_rate_after: CHANGE-ME ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed.\n\n- **Checks:** `CDH_CMD_ACC_CNT`, `PLD_RATE`\n- **Records:** `pld_rate_after`\n- **Applicability:** Applicable when PROC-001-S13 was executed.\n\n### PROC-001-S15 — EPS Officer · LOS - 2 min\n\nTake the final state snapshot: mode, state of charge, load current, and the trend\nagainst plan.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Packet Viewer, target HUGINN, packet HK\n\n**You know it worked when:** One final read of the whole HK packet, two minutes before LOS. This is the last state anyone will have until the next contact, which is why the step exists and why omitting it is an anomaly rather than a shrug.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-001-S15` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-001-S15 role: EPS operator: YOUR NAME state: completed records: soc_pct_at_los: CHANGE-ME mode_at_los: CHANGE-ME ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed.\n\n- **Checks:** `EPS_MODE`, `EPS_BATT_SOC`, `EPS_LOAD_I`, `EPS_BATT_V`\n- **Records:** `soc_pct_at_los`, `mode_at_los`\n- **Applicability:** always. A completion record entry reading `not_applicable` for this step is itself a finding.\n\n### PROC-001-S16 — Ops Planner · LOS\n\nClose the contact and open the pass report. Record the observed LOS against the computed\nwindow: \"began the report\" is an act with no observable, and a step whose completion is\nan assertion is one nobody can review.\n\n**Open:** campaign/CAMPAIGN_PLAN.md - the contact row for this pass\n\n**You know it worked when:** Telemetry stops updating: the contact has ended. Compare the wall-clock time it stopped against the LOS in the plan; they should agree within a few seconds.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-001-S16` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-001-S16 role: PLAN operator: YOUR NAME state: completed records: observed_los_utc: '2026-07-29T14:44:02Z' computed_los_utc: '2026-07-29T14:44:00Z' last_cdh_frame_cnt: 4515 ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed.\n\n- **Checks:** `CDH_FRAME_CNT`\n- **Records:** `observed_los_utc`, `computed_los_utc`, `last_cdh_frame_cnt`\n- **Applicability:** always. A completion record entry reading `not_applicable` for this step is itself a finding.\n\n### PROC-001-S17 — Ops Planner · LOS + 30 min\n\nFile the pass report to docs/pass_reports/, containing the pass plan, event timeline,\nlimit violations, actions with procedure step references, the per-step completion\nrecord, and the disposition of anomalies (HUG-OPS-002).\n\n```bash\npython tools/pass_report.py --validate docs/pass_reports/<pass-id>/report.yaml\n```\n\n**Open:** docs/pass_reports/<pass-id>/report.yaml\n\n**Run:**\n\n```bash\npython tools/pass_report.py --validate docs/pass_reports/<pass-id>/report.yaml\n```\n\n> `<pass-id>` — The pass identifier, form YYYY-MM-DD-Pn, read off campaign/CAMPAIGN_PLAN.md. The campaign's first pass is 2026-07-29-P1.\n\n**You know it worked when:** `[PASS]` and a line confirming the record accounts for all 18 steps. If it prints findings, each names the step and what is missing - fix those entries and run it again. A report is filed when it validates, not when you have stopped typing.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-001-S17` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-001-S17 role: PLAN operator: YOUR NAME state: completed ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed.\n\n- **Checks:** —\n- **Applicability:** always. A completion record entry reading `not_applicable` for this step is itself a finding.\n\n### PROC-001-S18 — Ops Planner · post-pass\n\nReview the completion record against this procedure. A step with no entry, or an\napplicable step without a completed or omitted entry, is an omission finding — record\nand disposition it as an anomaly with corrective action.\n\n**Open:** procedures/PROC-001.md - this document, beside your completed report\n\n**Run:**\n\n```bash\npython tools/pass_report.py --validate docs/pass_reports/<pass-id>/report.yaml\n```\n\n> `<pass-id>` — The pass identifier, form YYYY-MM-DD-Pn, read off campaign/CAMPAIGN_PLAN.md. The campaign's first pass is 2026-07-29-P1.\n\n**You know it worked when:** Read your record against this procedure, step by step. Any step with no entry, or an entry that claims more than you observed, is an omission finding and is recorded as one - `state: omitted` with an `anomaly:` reference, never deleted and never quietly upgraded.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-001-S18` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-001-S18 role: PLAN operator: YOUR NAME state: completed branch: complete observation: what you saw that made you choose that branch ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed. `branch:` must be one of: 'complete', 'omission finding'.\n\n- **Checks:** —\n- **Applicability:** always. A completion record entry reading `not_applicable` for this step is itself a finding.\n\n**Decision — Does the completion record account for every step of the governing procedure?**\n\n| Outcome | The observation that selects it | Then |\n|---|---|---|\n| `complete` | tools/pass_report.py --validate exits zero: exactly one entry per step, every not-applicable entry carrying a basis, every completed decision step carrying its branch-selecting observation. | File the report. Post-pass review is closed. |\n| `omission finding` | The validator names at least one step with no entry, a duplicate entry, a missing basis, or a decision entry with no branch-selecting observation. | Record the omission truthfully as an omitted entry — it is permanent for this pass and is never rewritten to completed. Disposition it as an anomaly with corrective action: re-execution at the next opportunity, recorded as a completed entry for the re-executed step and referenced from the omitted entry, or an explicit owner risk acceptance approved by the design authority and referenced from the omitted entry. |\n\n> A completed entry for this step must record **which branch was taken and the observation that selected it**. An omitted entry must record that no observation was made and no branch was selected — those are different facts and the record keeps them apart (HUG-OPS-002).\n\n","version":"0.1"},{"attachments":[],"id":"PROC-002","sha256":"sha256:894affab98219145c58bf8bf588f564085cee10d3233cac4589ab467615e384e","text":"# PROC-002 — Low-battery and limit response\n\nGENERATED FILE — DO NOT EDIT. Source: `defs/procedures.yaml`\nProcedures version: 0.1 · Dictionary sha256: 5e1c2750ef9e0d54…\n\n> Edit the source and regenerate. A hand edit here would make the document the\n> operator follows differ from the procedure `tools/pass_report.py` reviews the\n> completion record against — and post-pass review would then pass a pass in\n> which a step was never executed and never noticed (HUG-OPS-002).\n\n## Before you start\n\nYou do not need to know this program to run this procedure. Everything each\nstep needs is named in the step; this section names the things every step\nassumes.\n\n### The console — what to open\n\nThe ground system is OpenC3 COSMOS. Its console is a WEB PAGE, not a desktop\napplication: open **http://localhost:2910** in a browser. That page is \"the ground\nsystem\" wherever these procedures say it.\n\nNavigation is the **menu sidebar on the left**. Three tools there are used below:\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n|---|---|\n| Packet Viewer | Read the current value of a telemetry point. Target HUGINN, packet HK. |\n| Telemetry Grapher | Watch a point over time. Use this wherever a step says trend. |\n| Command Sender | Send a command. Target HUGINN. |\n\n### The terminal\n\nCommands shown as `python tools/...` are typed in a terminal. On Windows use\nPowerShell or Git Bash. The terminal's working directory must be the repository\nroot - the folder containing `BUILD_HANDOFF.md`.\n\nIf `python` is not found, or a command fails on a missing module, see\n`gnd/RUNBOOK.md` under \"Operator environment\" for which interpreter to use and how\nto install what is missing. To check the environment before you need it:\n\n```\npython tools/check_deps.py --preflight\n```\n\n### The pass plan\n\n**The pass plan and the campaign plan are the same document.** It is\n`campaign/CAMPAIGN_PLAN.md`. The pass plan is the part of it that concerns the pass\nyou are flying: the PASS-n section, plus the row for that pass's contact in the\ncomputed-week table.\n\nIf the file is not there, produce it:\n\n```\npython tools/gen_campaign.py\n```\n\n### The pass identifier\n\nEvery pass has an identifier of the form `YYYY-MM-DD-Pn` - the UTC date of the\ncontact, then the pass number from the campaign plan. The campaign's first pass is\n`2026-07-29-P1`.\n\nYou do not invent it; you read it off the campaign plan. Use the same string in\nthree places: the `pass_setup` command, the report directory name, and the\n`pass_id:` field inside the report.\n\n### Where recorded values go\n\nEverything a step lists under **Records** goes into one file: your pass report.\nCreate it before the pass.\n\n1. Open a terminal (PowerShell or Git Bash) and change to the repository root -\n   the folder containing `BUILD_HANDOFF.md`.\n\n2. Create the directory for this pass. Replace `2026-07-29-P1` with your pass\n   identifier here and in every command that follows.\n\n   ```\n   mkdir -p docs/pass_reports/2026-07-29-P1\n   ```\n\n3. Create the report from the template. **This is one command on one line**; if it\n   appears wrapped on your screen, it is still one line.\n\n   ```\n   python tools/pass_report.py --template PROC-001 > docs/pass_reports/2026-07-29-P1/report.yaml\n   ```\n\n4. Open `docs/pass_reports/2026-07-29-P1/report.yaml` in a text editor. It already\n   contains one block per step, in order, each reading `state: CHANGE-ME`.\n\n5. As you execute each step, find the block whose `step:` matches it and fill it in.\n   Each step below shows you the exact lines to write.\n\nTimes are written in UTC, in quotes, ending in Z - `'2026-07-29T14:36:30Z'`. Numbers\nare written without units.\n\n### Checking your record\n\nCheck your report as often as you like, and always before filing it. **This is one\ncommand on one line**, even if your screen wraps it:\n\n```\npython tools/pass_report.py --validate docs/pass_reports/2026-07-29-P1/report.yaml\n```\n\nIt prints `[PASS]`, or it names every step whose block is missing, unfilled or\ninconsistent. A report straight from the template FAILS - every step still reads\n`CHANGE-ME` - so a report nobody filled in cannot be mistaken for one that was.\n\n### Evidence\n\nDo not copy files into `verification/evidence/` by hand. Demonstrations write there\nas they run. If you find yourself moving a file into that directory, stop and ask -\na file placed there by hand will not verify.\n\n## Governs\n\nThe response to a low state of charge, a low battery voltage, a load-current excursion,\nor a SAFE annunciation. CONOPS Sec. 4.4 maps each alarmed limit to this procedure or to\nthe generic contingency response.\n\n## Roles\n\n| Role | Who |\n|---|---|\n| **EPS** | EPS Officer |\n| **FD** | Flight Director |\n\nEvery step below carries a stable identifier (HUG-OPS-001). **The completion\nrecord is keyed by these identifiers**, one entry per step, with no exceptions\n(HUG-OPS-002) — including the steps that turn out not to apply.\n\n## Steps\n\n### PROC-002-S01 — EPS Officer · on annunciation\n\nConfirm the annunciation: which point, which limit side, and the value read. Confirm it\nagainst the dictionary of record rather than against memory.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Packet Viewer; the annunciated point is the one shown yellow or red\n\n**You know it worked when:** Read the point's mnemonic, its current value, and which limit side it crossed. The dictionary's limits are in docs/tlm_cmd_dictionary.md if you want the thresholds beside the reading.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-002-S01` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-002-S01 role: EPS operator: YOUR NAME state: completed records: annunciated_point: EPS_BATT_SOC annunciated_value: 34.6 annunciated_level: YELLOW ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed.\n\n- **Checks:** `EPS_BATT_SOC`, `EPS_BATT_V`, `EPS_LOAD_I`, `EPS_BATT_I`, `EPS_MODE`\n- **Records:** `annunciated_point`, `annunciated_value`, `annunciated_level`\n- **Applicability:** always. A completion record entry reading `not_applicable` for this step is itself a finding.\n\n### PROC-002-S02 — EPS Officer · on annunciation\n\nDetermine whether the energy balance is negative.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Telemetry Grapher, EPS_BATT_I over the last few minutes\n\n**You know it worked when:** Negative balance: EPS_BATT_I sits below zero (discharging) while ADM_ECLIPSE reads SUNLIT, or the SoC trace slopes down. A single reading cannot tell you this - it is a trend, which is what the grapher is for.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-002-S02` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-002-S02 role: EPS operator: YOUR NAME state: completed branch: negative beyond eclipse observation: what you saw that made you choose that branch ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed. `branch:` must be one of: 'negative beyond eclipse', 'explained by eclipse'.\n\n- **Checks:** `EPS_BATT_I`, `EPS_SA1_I`, `EPS_SA2_I`, `ADM_ECLIPSE`, `EPS_LOAD_I`\n- **Applicability:** always. A completion record entry reading `not_applicable` for this step is itself a finding.\n\n**Decision — Is the spacecraft trending energy-negative beyond what the illumination state explains?**\n\n| Outcome | The observation that selects it | Then |\n|---|---|---|\n| `negative beyond eclipse` | EPS_BATT_I is negative across three or more consecutive frames while ADM_ECLIPSE reads SUNLIT, or the discharge is deeper than the eclipse plan predicts. | Continue to PROC-002-S03. |\n| `explained by eclipse` | EPS_BATT_I is negative while ADM_ECLIPSE reads ECLIPSE and the depth of discharge tracks the plan. | No shed action. Monitor to eclipse exit and confirm recovery; if state of charge does not recover after exit, return to PROC-002-S02. |\n\n> A completed entry for this step must record **which branch was taken and the observation that selected it**. An omitted entry must record that no observation was made and no branch was selected — those are different facts and the record keeps them apart (HUG-OPS-002).\n\n### PROC-002-S03 — EPS Officer · on negative trend\n\nIdentify the cause among the two the record anticipates.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Packet Viewer, EPS_SA1_I and EPS_SA2_I side by side, and EPS_LOAD_I\n\n**You know it worked when:** String loss: one array current near zero while the other holds - an ASYMMETRY. Stuck load: both strings normal but EPS_LOAD_I about 2 W higher than the plan expects. These are the two causes the record anticipates and they look different on the same screen.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-002-S03` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-002-S03 role: EPS operator: YOUR NAME state: completed branch: array-string failure observation: what you saw that made you choose that branch ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed. `branch:` must be one of: 'array-string failure', 'load excursion', 'neither'.\n\n- **Checks:** `EPS_SA1_I`, `EPS_SA2_I`, `EPS_LOAD_I`\n- **Applicability:** Applicable when PROC-002-S02 selected the negative branch.\n\n**Decision — Is generation down, or is load up?**\n\n| Outcome | The observation that selects it | Then |\n|---|---|---|\n| `array-string failure` | EPS_SA1_I and EPS_SA2_I are asymmetric — one string at or near zero while the other carries its expected current for the illumination state. | Continue to PROC-002-S04. Generation cannot be restored from the ground; the response is load management. |\n| `load excursion` | EPS_LOAD_I is at or above 1.5 A with both array strings symmetric and consistent with illumination. | Continue to PROC-002-S04, load-shed branch. |\n| `neither` | Strings symmetric and EPS_LOAD_I below 1.5 A, yet the discharge persists. | Declare an anomaly (FD). The generic contingency response governs: safe the spacecraft by command if power integrity is threatened, and file an anomaly report. |\n\n> A completed entry for this step must record **which branch was taken and the observation that selected it**. An omitted entry must record that no observation was made and no branch was selected — those are different facts and the record keeps them apart (HUG-OPS-002).\n\n### PROC-002-S04 — EPS Officer · on negative trend\n\nShed the payload: send CMD_PLD_SET_STATE with state OFF. This is the only load the\nground can shed.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Command Sender, CMD_PLD_SET_STATE, state OFF\n\n**You know it worked when:** The command is sent. Note the time to the second; S05 verifies against it.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-002-S04` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-002-S04 role: EPS operator: YOUR NAME state: completed records: shed_command_utc: '2026-07-29T14:39:10Z' ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed.\n\n- **Checks:** —\n- **Records:** `shed_command_utc`\n- **Applicability:** Applicable when PROC-002-S03 selected array-string failure or load excursion. Not applicable when the payload is already OFF — state PLD_STATE as the basis.\n\n### PROC-002-S05 — EPS Officer · after shed\n\nVerify the shed by counter delta and affected point.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Packet Viewer, PLD_STATE and EPS_LOAD_I\n\n**You know it worked when:** PLD_STATE reads OFF and EPS_LOAD_I drops by roughly the payload load, within one frame. Read EPS_LOAD_I BEFORE the send too - the drop is the evidence, and a drop needs a before.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-002-S05` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-002-S05 role: EPS operator: YOUR NAME state: completed records: pld_state_after_shed: OFF load_i_after_shed: 0.62 ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed.\n\n- **Checks:** `CDH_CMD_ACC_CNT`, `PLD_STATE`, `EPS_LOAD_I`\n- **Records:** `pld_state_after_shed`, `load_i_after_shed`\n- **Applicability:** Applicable when PROC-002-S04 was executed.\n\n### PROC-002-S06 — EPS Officer · after shed\n\nDetermine whether shedding restored a non-negative balance.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Telemetry Grapher, EPS_BATT_I\n\n**You know it worked when:** Restored: EPS_BATT_I is at or above zero after the shed. Not restored: still negative, and the ladder will act on its own at 30 %.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-002-S06` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-002-S06 role: EPS operator: YOUR NAME state: completed branch: recovered observation: what you saw that made you choose that branch ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed. `branch:` must be one of: 'recovered', 'still negative'.\n\n- **Checks:** `EPS_BATT_I`, `EPS_BATT_SOC`\n- **Applicability:** Applicable when PROC-002-S04 was executed.\n\n**Decision — Is EPS_BATT_I non-negative after the shed?**\n\n| Outcome | The observation that selects it | Then |\n|---|---|---|\n| `recovered` | EPS_BATT_I is at or above zero across three or more consecutive frames while sunlit. | Continue to PROC-002-S09. The stuck-load case reaches here with the balance still negative and is handled by the next branch. |\n| `still negative` | EPS_BATT_I remains negative across three or more consecutive frames after the shed was verified — the load did not go away when the payload did, which is the stuck-load presentation. | Continue to PROC-002-S07. |\n\n> A completed entry for this step must record **which branch was taken and the observation that selected it**. An omitted entry must record that no observation was made and no branch was selected — those are different facts and the record keeps them apart (HUG-OPS-002).\n\n### PROC-002-S07 — Flight Director · on continued discharge\n\nDo not fight the mode ladder. The spacecraft protects itself autonomously: LOW_POWER at\n30 % and SAFE at 20 %, shedding the payload on entry. Commanding against a descending\nladder spends the margin the ladder exists to hold.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Packet Viewer, EPS_MODE and EPS_BATT_SOC\n\n**You know it worked when:** You are watching, not commanding. EPS_MODE changes to LOW_POWER at 30 % or SAFE at 20 % on its own. Record the mode it entered and the SoC when it did; commanding against a descending ladder spends the margin the ladder exists to hold.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-002-S07` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-002-S07 role: FD operator: YOUR NAME state: completed records: mode_entered: LOW_POWER soc_pct_at_entry: 29.8 ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed.\n\n- **Checks:** `EPS_BATT_SOC`, `EPS_MODE`\n- **Records:** `mode_entered`, `soc_pct_at_entry`\n- **Applicability:** Applicable when PROC-002-S06 selected the still-negative branch. Not applicable when state of charge recovered before an autonomous entry — state the recovering SoC as the basis.\n\n### PROC-002-S08 — Flight Director · on continued discharge\n\nDetermine whether the spacecraft must be safed by command.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Packet Viewer, EPS_MODE and EPS_BATT_SOC\n\n**You know it worked when:** Safe by command if the ladder has not acted and SoC continues to fall; hold if the ladder has already taken the spacecraft where you would have sent it.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-002-S08` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-002-S08 role: FD operator: YOUR NAME state: completed branch: safe by command observation: what you saw that made you choose that branch ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed. `branch:` must be one of: 'safe by command', 'ladder holds'.\n\n- **Checks:** `EPS_BATT_SOC`, `EPS_BATT_V`, `EPS_BUS_V`, `EPS_MODE`\n- **Applicability:** Applicable when PROC-002-S06 selected the still-negative branch.\n\n**Decision — Is power integrity threatened beyond what the ladder will hold?**\n\n| Outcome | The observation that selects it | Then |\n|---|---|---|\n| `safe by command` | EPS_BATT_SOC at or below 20 % with the ladder not yet in SAFE, or EPS_BUS_V outside 4.75 to 5.25 V. | Send CMD_SAFE_ENTER. It is hazardous: the ground system requires the two-step confirmation, and the second step is the resend that follows the caught refusal, never a bypass called on its own. |\n| `ladder holds` | EPS_MODE has entered LOW_POWER or SAFE autonomously and EPS_BATT_SOC has stopped falling across three or more consecutive frames. | Continue to PROC-002-S09. |\n\n> A completed entry for this step must record **which branch was taken and the observation that selected it**. An omitted entry must record that no observation was made and no branch was selected — those are different facts and the record keeps them apart (HUG-OPS-002).\n\n### PROC-002-S09 — Flight Director · on recovery\n\nRecover the commanded mode. Recovery from LOW_POWER or SAFE is a deliberate ground act\nand is gated on the recovery floor for the target mode, not on the autonomous entry\nthreshold — the floors sit above the entries so a recovery cannot immediately re-trip.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Command Sender, CMD_MODE_SET\n\n**You know it worked when:** Recovery has a FLOOR: the command is accepted only above the recovery floor for the target mode, and rejected with MODE_INHIBIT below it. A rejection here is the spacecraft protecting itself, not a failed command.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-002-S09` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-002-S09 role: FD operator: YOUR NAME state: completed branch: recover to NOMINAL observation: what you saw that made you choose that branch ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed. `branch:` must be one of: 'recover to NOMINAL', 'recover to LOW_POWER', 'hold'.\n\n- **Checks:** `EPS_BATT_SOC`, `EPS_MODE`\n- **Applicability:** Applicable when the spacecraft is in LOW_POWER or SAFE. Not applicable when EPS_MODE still reads NOMINAL — state that as the basis.\n\n**Decision — Is state of charge at or above the recovery floor for the target mode?**\n\n| Outcome | The observation that selects it | Then |\n|---|---|---|\n| `recover to NOMINAL` | EPS_BATT_SOC at or above 35 %. | Send CMD_MODE_SET with target NOMINAL, then PROC-002-S10. |\n| `recover to LOW_POWER` | EPS_BATT_SOC at or above 25 % but below 35 %. | Send CMD_MODE_SET with target LOW_POWER, then PROC-002-S10. |\n| `hold` | EPS_BATT_SOC below 25 %. | Do not command a recovery. Hold and monitor; a mode command below the floor is refused with MODE_INHIBIT and spends a command slot for nothing. |\n\n> A completed entry for this step must record **which branch was taken and the observation that selected it**. An omitted entry must record that no observation was made and no branch was selected — those are different facts and the record keeps them apart (HUG-OPS-002).\n\n### PROC-002-S10 — EPS Officer · on recovery\n\nVerify the mode change by counter delta and affected point.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Packet Viewer, CDH_CMD_ACC_CNT and EPS_MODE\n\n**You know it worked when:** Accept counter +1 and EPS_MODE reading the mode you commanded, within one frame.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-002-S10` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-002-S10 role: EPS operator: YOUR NAME state: completed records: mode_after_recovery: NOMINAL ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed.\n\n- **Checks:** `CDH_CMD_ACC_CNT`, `CDH_CMD_REJ_CNT`, `CDH_REJ_REASON`, `EPS_MODE`\n- **Records:** `mode_after_recovery`\n- **Applicability:** Applicable when PROC-002-S09 sent a mode command.\n\n### PROC-002-S11 — Flight Director · closeout\n\nDeclare the anomaly and file the anomaly report. Payload re-activation is not part of\nthis procedure: it is a PROC-001 commanding step on a later pass, subject to the 40 %\nactivation inhibit.\n\n**Open:** docs/pass_reports/<pass-id>/report.yaml - the anomalies: section\n\n**Run:**\n\n```bash\npython tools/pass_report.py --validate docs/pass_reports/<pass-id>/report.yaml\n```\n\n> `<pass-id>` — The pass identifier, form YYYY-MM-DD-Pn, read off campaign/CAMPAIGN_PLAN.md. The campaign's first pass is 2026-07-29-P1.\n\n**You know it worked when:** Every anomaly entry needs an id, the step it arose at, the finding, a feasibility statement and a disposition. The validator names any that are incomplete.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-002-S11` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-002-S11 role: FD operator: YOUR NAME state: completed ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed.\n\n- **Checks:** `EPS_MODE`, `EPS_BATT_SOC`\n- **Applicability:** always. A completion record entry reading `not_applicable` for this step is itself a finding.\n\n","version":"0.1"},{"attachments":[],"id":"PROC-003","sha256":"sha256:3c5b94823ab160e8f4da7fdb28c849237b5e3b2edbd9ec6e082f95227defa609","text":"# PROC-003 — Loss of telemetry or commandability\n\nGENERATED FILE — DO NOT EDIT. Source: `defs/procedures.yaml`\nProcedures version: 0.1 · Dictionary sha256: 5e1c2750ef9e0d54…\n\n> Edit the source and regenerate. A hand edit here would make the document the\n> operator follows differ from the procedure `tools/pass_report.py` reviews the\n> completion record against — and post-pass review would then pass a pass in\n> which a step was never executed and never noticed (HUG-OPS-002).\n\n## Before you start\n\nYou do not need to know this program to run this procedure. Everything each\nstep needs is named in the step; this section names the things every step\nassumes.\n\n### The console — what to open\n\nThe ground system is OpenC3 COSMOS. Its console is a WEB PAGE, not a desktop\napplication: open **http://localhost:2910** in a browser. That page is \"the ground\nsystem\" wherever these procedures say it.\n\nNavigation is the **menu sidebar on the left**. Three tools there are used below:\n\n| Tool | Use it to |\n|---|---|\n| Packet Viewer | Read the current value of a telemetry point. Target HUGINN, packet HK. |\n| Telemetry Grapher | Watch a point over time. Use this wherever a step says trend. |\n| Command Sender | Send a command. Target HUGINN. |\n\n### The terminal\n\nCommands shown as `python tools/...` are typed in a terminal. On Windows use\nPowerShell or Git Bash. The terminal's working directory must be the repository\nroot - the folder containing `BUILD_HANDOFF.md`.\n\nIf `python` is not found, or a command fails on a missing module, see\n`gnd/RUNBOOK.md` under \"Operator environment\" for which interpreter to use and how\nto install what is missing. To check the environment before you need it:\n\n```\npython tools/check_deps.py --preflight\n```\n\n### The pass plan\n\n**The pass plan and the campaign plan are the same document.** It is\n`campaign/CAMPAIGN_PLAN.md`. The pass plan is the part of it that concerns the pass\nyou are flying: the PASS-n section, plus the row for that pass's contact in the\ncomputed-week table.\n\nIf the file is not there, produce it:\n\n```\npython tools/gen_campaign.py\n```\n\n### The pass identifier\n\nEvery pass has an identifier of the form `YYYY-MM-DD-Pn` - the UTC date of the\ncontact, then the pass number from the campaign plan. The campaign's first pass is\n`2026-07-29-P1`.\n\nYou do not invent it; you read it off the campaign plan. Use the same string in\nthree places: the `pass_setup` command, the report directory name, and the\n`pass_id:` field inside the report.\n\n### Where recorded values go\n\nEverything a step lists under **Records** goes into one file: your pass report.\nCreate it before the pass.\n\n1. Open a terminal (PowerShell or Git Bash) and change to the repository root -\n   the folder containing `BUILD_HANDOFF.md`.\n\n2. Create the directory for this pass. Replace `2026-07-29-P1` with your pass\n   identifier here and in every command that follows.\n\n   ```\n   mkdir -p docs/pass_reports/2026-07-29-P1\n   ```\n\n3. Create the report from the template. **This is one command on one line**; if it\n   appears wrapped on your screen, it is still one line.\n\n   ```\n   python tools/pass_report.py --template PROC-001 > docs/pass_reports/2026-07-29-P1/report.yaml\n   ```\n\n4. Open `docs/pass_reports/2026-07-29-P1/report.yaml` in a text editor. It already\n   contains one block per step, in order, each reading `state: CHANGE-ME`.\n\n5. As you execute each step, find the block whose `step:` matches it and fill it in.\n   Each step below shows you the exact lines to write.\n\nTimes are written in UTC, in quotes, ending in Z - `'2026-07-29T14:36:30Z'`. Numbers\nare written without units.\n\n### Checking your record\n\nCheck your report as often as you like, and always before filing it. **This is one\ncommand on one line**, even if your screen wraps it:\n\n```\npython tools/pass_report.py --validate docs/pass_reports/2026-07-29-P1/report.yaml\n```\n\nIt prints `[PASS]`, or it names every step whose block is missing, unfilled or\ninconsistent. A report straight from the template FAILS - every step still reads\n`CHANGE-ME` - so a report nobody filled in cannot be mistaken for one that was.\n\n### Evidence\n\nDo not copy files into `verification/evidence/` by hand. Demonstrations write there\nas they run. If you find yourself moving a file into that directory, stop and ask -\na file placed there by hand will not verify.\n\n## Governs\n\nTwo presentations of lost insight: telemetry stopped, and commands unacknowledged. They\nshare a procedure because the escalation is the same and because the two can be present\nat once — uplink is independent of the housekeeping downlink (HUG-EPS-004(c)), which is\nthe whole reason an escalation path exists.\n\n## Roles\n\n| Role | Who |\n|---|---|\n| **EPS** | EPS Officer |\n| **FD** | Flight Director |\n\nEvery step below carries a stable identifier (HUG-OPS-001). **The completion\nrecord is keyed by these identifiers**, one entry per step, with no exceptions\n(HUG-OPS-002) — including the steps that turn out not to apply.\n\n## Steps\n\n### PROC-003-S01 — EPS Officer · on staleness annunciation\n\nRecord the staleness annunciation and the last frame received. The ground-side monitor\nannunciates YELLOW at 10 s and RED at 30 s of no advance; a halted counter cannot alarm\nitself, which is why the monitor is ground-side.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Packet Viewer, CDH_FRAME_CNT and the STALENESS target\n\n**You know it worked when:** The staleness annunciator reads YELLOW (10 s of no advance) or RED (30 s). Record the last frame counter value you saw and when you saw it - after this the number stops changing, so the time you noticed it is part of the record.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-003-S01` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-003-S01 role: EPS operator: YOUR NAME state: completed records: last_frame_utc: '2026-07-29T14:38:05Z' last_cdh_frame_cnt: 4515 staleness_level: RED ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed.\n\n- **Checks:** `CDH_FRAME_CNT`\n- **Records:** `last_frame_utc`, `last_cdh_frame_cnt`, `staleness_level`\n- **Applicability:** Applicable on the telemetry presentation.\n\n### PROC-003-S02 — EPS Officer · on staleness annunciation\n\nDetermine whether the staleness is a ground-side artifact before escalating to the\nspacecraft. Escalating on a ground fault sends a hazardous command to a healthy vehicle.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - the interface state, and the STALENESS annunciator level\n\n**You know it worked when:** Spacecraft-side: the interface still reads CONNECTED and the annunciator reads a staleness LEVEL. Ground-side: the interface is not CONNECTED. Monitor read fault: the annunciator reads READ_FAULT or NO_READ - which is a statement about the ground segment, not about the spacecraft, and must not be read as staleness.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-003-S02` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-003-S02 role: EPS operator: YOUR NAME state: completed branch: spacecraft-side observation: what you saw that made you choose that branch ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed. `branch:` must be one of: 'spacecraft-side', 'ground-side', 'monitor read fault'.\n\n- **Checks:** `CDH_FRAME_CNT`\n- **Applicability:** Applicable on the telemetry presentation.\n\n**Decision — Which of the three conditions that all present as \"the counter is not advancing at the ground\" is this?**\n\n| Outcome | The observation that selects it | Then |\n|---|---|---|\n| `spacecraft-side` | The staleness annunciator reads YELLOW or RED, the ground system's interface reads CONNECTED, and its received count is frozen — an absence of frames on a live link. | Continue to PROC-003-S03. |\n| `ground-side` | The interface reads other than CONNECTED, or its received count is advancing while the displayed counter is not. | Restore the ground path and re-run PROC-001-S02. Do not command the spacecraft: nothing observed says anything about it. |\n| `monitor read fault` | The staleness annunciator reads READ_FAULT or NO_READ rather than YELLOW or RED. | The monitor is blind and its silence is about itself, not about the spacecraft. Restart the staleness monitor and re-acquire; escalating here would send a hazardous command to a vehicle nothing has observed. READ_FAULT means reads began failing after succeeding; NO_READ before AOS is the ordinary pre-acquisition state and is not an anomaly at all. |\n\n> A completed entry for this step must record **which branch was taken and the observation that selected it**. An omitted entry must record that no observation was made and no branch was selected — those are different facts and the record keeps them apart (HUG-OPS-002).\n\n### PROC-003-S03 — Flight Director · on confirmed spacecraft-side loss\n\nAuthorize and send CMD_SYS_RESET. It is hazardous: the ground system requires the\ntwo-step confirmation, and the second step is the resend that follows the caught\nrefusal.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Command Sender, CMD_SYS_RESET\n\n**You know it worked when:** CMD_SYS_RESET is HAZARDOUS: the Command Sender asks for confirmation before it sends. That two-step is deliberate (B7) - read the command back before confirming.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-003-S03` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-003-S03 role: FD operator: YOUR NAME state: completed records: reset_command_utc: '2026-07-29T14:39:44Z' ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed.\n\n- **Checks:** —\n- **Records:** `reset_command_utc`\n- **Applicability:** Applicable when PROC-003-S02 selected the spacecraft-side branch.\n\n### PROC-003-S04 — EPS Officer · after reset\n\nVerify the reset by its post-reset counter epoch, NOT by delta arithmetic. CMD_SYS_RESET\nis the sole exception: it reinitializes the counters and then applies its own\nacceptance, so the first post-reset frame reads CDH_CMD_ACC_CNT = 1 and CDH_CMD_REJ_CNT\n= 0. That epoch IS the acknowledgement.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Packet Viewer, CDH_CMD_ACC_CNT, CDH_CMD_REJ_CNT, EPS_BATT_SOC\n\n**You know it worked when:** The post-reset epoch: CDH_CMD_ACC_CNT reads exactly 1 and CDH_CMD_REJ_CNT exactly 0 - NOT the previous values plus one. Reset is the sole exception to counter delta arithmetic, so do not compute a delta here. State of charge is preserved across the reset.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-003-S04` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-003-S04 role: EPS operator: YOUR NAME state: completed records: acc_cnt_post_reset: 1 rej_cnt_post_reset: 0 soc_pct_post_reset: 38.9 ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed.\n\n- **Checks:** `CDH_CMD_ACC_CNT`, `CDH_CMD_REJ_CNT`, `CDH_FRAME_CNT`, `EPS_BATT_SOC`\n- **Records:** `acc_cnt_post_reset`, `rej_cnt_post_reset`, `soc_pct_post_reset`\n- **Applicability:** Applicable when PROC-003-S03 was executed.\n\n### PROC-003-S05 — EPS Officer · after reset\n\nDetermine whether telemetry has been restored.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Packet Viewer, CDH_FRAME_CNT\n\n**You know it worked when:** Restored: the frame counter is advancing again and the annunciator returns to GREEN. Not restored: it is still frozen after the reset, which escalates.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-003-S05` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-003-S05 role: EPS operator: YOUR NAME state: completed branch: restored observation: what you saw that made you choose that branch ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed. `branch:` must be one of: 'restored', 'not restored'.\n\n- **Checks:** `CDH_FRAME_CNT`, `EPS_BATT_SOC`\n- **Applicability:** Applicable when PROC-003-S03 was executed.\n\n**Decision — Is CDH_FRAME_CNT advancing again, with state of charge preserved?**\n\n| Outcome | The observation that selects it | Then |\n|---|---|---|\n| `restored` | CDH_FRAME_CNT advances across consecutive frames, the staleness annunciator returns to GREEN, and EPS_BATT_SOC is continuous with the value before the outage. | Return to PROC-001 at PROC-001-S06 and reassess state of health. |\n| `not restored` | No frames after the reset command, or the staleness annunciator remains RED past 30 s. | Declare the anomaly (FD). Insight is not recoverable this pass; file the anomaly report and plan the next contact against the last known state. |\n\n> A completed entry for this step must record **which branch was taken and the observation that selected it**. An omitted entry must record that no observation was made and no branch was selected — those are different facts and the record keeps them apart (HUG-OPS-002).\n\n### PROC-003-S06 — EPS Officer · on unacknowledged command\n\nEstablish that a verified send produced no accounting. Every received command increments\nexactly one counter by exactly one, visible no later than the next housekeeping frame;\nabsence of any delta within 10 s of a verified send is the loss-of-commandability\ndeclaration threshold (HUG-CMD-007).\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Command Sender, then Packet Viewer for both counters\n\n**You know it worked when:** Read CDH_CMD_ACC_CNT and CDH_CMD_REJ_CNT before the send, send, then watch both for 10 s. Establishing that NEITHER moved is the observation; you cannot make it without the before-values.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-003-S06` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-003-S06 role: EPS operator: YOUR NAME state: completed records: send_utc: '2026-07-29T14:40:12Z' acc_cnt_at_send: 4472 rej_cnt_at_send: 0 elapsed_s: 10 ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed.\n\n- **Checks:** `CDH_CMD_ACC_CNT`, `CDH_CMD_REJ_CNT`, `CDH_FRAME_CNT`\n- **Records:** `send_utc`, `acc_cnt_at_send`, `rej_cnt_at_send`, `elapsed_s`\n- **Applicability:** Applicable on the command presentation.\n\n### PROC-003-S07 — EPS Officer · on unacknowledged command\n\nDetermine whether the command path is lost.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Packet Viewer, both command counters\n\n**You know it worked when:** Command path lost: neither counter moved within 10 s of a verified send. Not lost: either counter moved, and the command was received even if it was rejected.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-003-S07` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-003-S07 role: EPS operator: YOUR NAME state: completed branch: accounted late observation: what you saw that made you choose that branch ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed. `branch:` must be one of: 'accounted late', 'not accounted'.\n\n- **Checks:** `CDH_CMD_ACC_CNT`, `CDH_CMD_REJ_CNT`, `CDH_FRAME_CNT`\n- **Applicability:** Applicable on the command presentation.\n\n**Decision — Did either counter change within 10 s of the verified send?**\n\n| Outcome | The observation that selects it | Then |\n|---|---|---|\n| `accounted late` | One counter incremented by exactly one within 10 s, later than the next frame but inside the threshold. | Not a loss of commandability. Record the latency in the pass report and continue the governing procedure. |\n| `not accounted` | Neither counter changed within 10 s of the verified send, while CDH_FRAME_CNT continues to advance — telemetry is flowing and commands are not being accounted. | Continue to PROC-003-S08. |\n\n> A completed entry for this step must record **which branch was taken and the observation that selected it**. An omitted entry must record that no observation was made and no branch was selected — those are different facts and the record keeps them apart (HUG-OPS-002).\n\n### PROC-003-S08 — EPS Officer · on unacknowledged command\n\nRetry once within the pass. A single retry distinguishes a lost uplink frame from a lost\ncommand path, and costs one command slot.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Command Sender\n\n**You know it worked when:** Send the same command once more. One retry distinguishes a lost uplink frame from a lost command path; a second and third tell you nothing the first did not.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-003-S08` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-003-S08 role: EPS operator: YOUR NAME state: completed records: retry_utc: '2026-07-29T14:40:31Z' retry_outcome: unacknowledged ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed.\n\n- **Checks:** `CDH_CMD_ACC_CNT`, `CDH_CMD_REJ_CNT`\n- **Records:** `retry_utc`, `retry_outcome`\n- **Applicability:** Applicable when PROC-003-S07 selected the not-accounted branch.\n\n### PROC-003-S09 — Flight Director · on failed retry\n\nDetermine whether to escalate to a reset.\n\n**Open:** http://localhost:2910 - Packet Viewer, both command counters\n\n**You know it worked when:** Escalate if the retry was also unacknowledged. Do not escalate if the retry was accounted - the path is there.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-003-S09` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-003-S09 role: FD operator: YOUR NAME state: completed branch: retry accounted observation: what you saw that made you choose that branch ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed. `branch:` must be one of: 'retry accounted', 'retry unaccounted'.\n\n- **Checks:** `CDH_CMD_ACC_CNT`, `CDH_CMD_REJ_CNT`, `CDH_FRAME_CNT`\n- **Applicability:** Applicable when PROC-003-S08 was executed.\n\n**Decision — Did the retry produce a counter delta?**\n\n| Outcome | The observation that selects it | Then |\n|---|---|---|\n| `retry accounted` | One counter incremented by exactly one after the retry. | The command path is live. Return to the governing procedure and record the first send as an anomaly in the pass report. |\n| `retry unaccounted` | Neither counter changed within 10 s of the retry, while CDH_FRAME_CNT continues to advance. | Escalate to PROC-003-S03: send CMD_SYS_RESET under two-step confirmation. Verify by the post-reset counter epoch at PROC-003-S04, not by delta. |\n\n> A completed entry for this step must record **which branch was taken and the observation that selected it**. An omitted entry must record that no observation was made and no branch was selected — those are different facts and the record keeps them apart (HUG-OPS-002).\n\n### PROC-003-S10 — Flight Director · closeout\n\nDeclare the anomaly and file the anomaly report, whichever branch was taken. A recovered\noutage is still an outage and is still reported.\n\n**Open:** docs/pass_reports/<pass-id>/report.yaml - the anomalies: section\n\n**Run:**\n\n```bash\npython tools/pass_report.py --validate docs/pass_reports/<pass-id>/report.yaml\n```\n\n> `<pass-id>` — The pass identifier, form YYYY-MM-DD-Pn, read off campaign/CAMPAIGN_PLAN.md. The campaign's first pass is 2026-07-29-P1.\n\n**You know it worked when:** The anomaly report is part of the pass report, not a separate document. The validator names any anomaly entry missing its finding, feasibility or disposition.\n\n**Write it down:** In your report, find the block that begins `- step: PROC-003-S10` and make it read: ```yaml - step: PROC-003-S10 role: FD operator: YOUR NAME state: completed ``` The values above are EXAMPLES showing the shape. Replace each with what you actually observed.\n\n- **Checks:** `EPS_MODE`, `EPS_BATT_SOC`, `CDH_FRAME_CNT`\n- **Applicability:** always. A completion record entry reading `not_applicable` for this step is itself a finding.\n\n","version":"0.1"}],"report_kind":"CHECK_IN","run_id":"PH2-PASS1-OPEN-R4","rung":"AI_AUTONOMOUS","rung_reason":null,"seat":"GROUND","triggers":[{"detail":{},"met_s":320,"type":"CHECK_IN"}],"turn_id":11,"turn_kind":"REPORT","uses_left":{"L-MODE-LOWPOWER":2,"L-MODE-NOMINAL":2},"utc":"2026-10-30T03:31:59.195Z"}